Modified by the Camoucrome authors, 2026-10-28 (GPLv3 section 5(a)). The
changes from Cursory 2.1.0:
  - a C++ port of the Python original;
  - the recorded trajectories repacked as int16/uint16 tables;
  - the random source is SplitMix64 instead of numpy's;
  - the movement time is fitted into [min, max] ms (the caller's bounds) and
    capped at 10 s;
  - points with equal millisecond offsets are de-duplicated;
  - the timing is resampled and given a 1 ms jitter floor, so offsets are
    strictly increasing.

